        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is the Treaflow walking pad worth it?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For slow desk walking on a budget, yes, within limits. The 3100W is light, low, pre-assembled and about $120, with a simple 8% peg incline. Its motor has no continuous rating, independent sources raise durability concerns, and the brand listing gives one year of warranty. Buy it for walking, from a seller whose return terms you have read.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Who makes Treaflow walking pads?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The brand listing names Treaflow as the manufacturer, and the pads carry model numbers such as 3100W and 3120. One independent reviewer reports that Treaflow is a sub-brand of a Chinese manufacturer called Abonow; we could not confirm that from the brand. The pads are sold mainly through third-party sellers on Amazon, and the brand has its own website.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How does the Treaflow incline work?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On the 3100W, pegs under the base fold out to tilt the deck, giving an incline described as 0 to 8 percent. There is no incline motor and the remote does not change it, so you set it with the belt stopped and you off the pad. The cheaper 3120 is listed with a fixed 8 percent incline that cannot be lowered.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the top speed of the Treaflow walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The brand listing and independent coverage agree on 4 mph for the 3100W. A few resale listings for what appears to be the same pad claim up to 6 mph; we could not reconcile those and they may describe another version. Minimum speed is given as either 0.5 or 0.6 mph depending on the source. It is a walking pad, not a running machine.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What warranty does Treaflow give?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The brand's Amazon listing gives one year, an independent write-up agrees, and the brand website as indexed advertises a one-year warranty. One third-party summary says two years. Plan around one year, and ask the seller how claims work and who pays shipping on a faulty unit before you order.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you use the Treaflow walking pad under a desk?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, that is its main purpose. It has no handlebar, is 4.3 inches tall, and is controlled by remote. Walk at a slow speed, keep the remote within reach, and break long sessions up. Many people find a flat deck easier for typing than the 8 percent tilt, so try both positions during the return window.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
